Sure! To do that you can use the `prioritizeAndSort` helper.  
Just feed it your array (as a JSON‑string), tell it that `"urgent"` is the priority status, and set `ascending` to `"true"`.

```javascript
// Suppose this is your original array:
const myItemList = [
  { name: "Alpha",   status: "normal" },
  { name: "Bravo",   status: "urgent" },
  { name: "Charlie", status: "normal" },
  { name: "Delta",   status: "urgent" }
];

// Call the helper
const sorted = prioritizeAndSort({
  items: JSON.stringify(myItemList),   // pass the array as a JSON string
  priorityStatus: "urgent",
  ascending: "true"
});

console.log(sorted);
// Expected output:
// [
//   { name: "Bravo",   status: "urgent" },
//   { name: "Delta",   status: "urgent" },
//   { name: "Alpha",   status: "normal" },
//   { name: "Charlie", status: "normal" }
// ]
```

If you already have `myItemList` defined in your code, just replace the example array above with your own. Let me know if you’d like help converting a specific list or if you run into any issues!
